\cdots &lt; w_k\)) with probabilities \(\{ μ_1, μ_2, \dots, μ_k \}\). It is easy to see that in this case the action \(a\) should be in the set \(\{ w_1, w_2, \dots,
w_k \}\).
To fix ideas, we repeat the above numerical example when \(\ALPHABET W = \{0, 1, \dots, 100\}\).

