Theorem 7.2 Assume that \(p &lt; c_s\). Define \[ σ_t = \arg \min_{z \in \reals} \bigl\{ f_t(z) \bigr\} . \] Then, \(σ_t \ge 0\), \[\begin{equation} \label{eq:V-lost}
V^*_t(s) = \begin{cases}
  f_t(σ_t) - ps , &\text{if } s \le σ_t \\
  f_t(s) - p s   , & \text{otherwise }
\end{cases}
\end{equation}\] and \[\begin{equation}\label{eq:pi-lost}
  π^*_t(s) = \begin{cases}
  σ_t - s, &\text{if } s \le σ_t \\
  0, & \text{otherwise }
\end{cases}\end{equation}\]

Note that we cannot follow the same proof argument as before because for a convex function \(\EXP[f([s-W]^{+})]\) is not necessarily convex. So we cannot show that \(H_t(z)\) is convex. So, we need a slightly different proof argument.






NoteProof





We will show the following fact.
Fact. If \(f_{t+1}\) is convex, then \(V^*_{t+1}, π^*_{t+1}\) is of the specified form and \[g_t(z) = V^*_{t+1}([z]^{+}) - c_s [z]^{-}\] is convex.
Implication of fact. Convexity of \(g_t\) implies that \(g_t(z) + c_h [z]^{+}\) is convex (since it is sum of two convex functions). Hence, \(f_t(z)\) is convex (since it is sum of a linear function and weighted average of convex functions). This implies the result holds by induction.
Proof of fact. The fact that convexity of \(f_{t+1}\) implies the structure of \(V^*_{t+1}\) and \(π^*_{t+1}\) follows from the same argument as Theorem 7.1. Then, by definition of \(g_t\), we have \[
g_t(z) = \begin{cases}
f_{t+1}(σ_{t+1}) - c_s z, & \hbox{if } z &lt; 0 \\
f_{t+1}(σ_{t+1}) - p z,   & \hbox{if } 0 \le z &lt; σ_{t+1} \\
f_{t+1}(z) - p z,   & \hbox{if } σ_{t+1} \le z
\end{cases}
\] In the range \((-∞, 0)\) the function is linear and has slope \(-c_s\); in the range \([0, σ_{t+1})\) the function is linear and has slope \(-p\); in the range \([σ_{t+1}, ∞)\), the function is convex and increasing and has a slope greater than \(-p\). Hence, the slope of \(g_t\) is increasing from \((-∞,∞)\) and hence the function is convex.
The above argument also shows that the slope of \(g_t(z)\) (and therefore of \(g_t(z) + c_h [z]^{+}\)) is \(-c_s\) in \((-∞,0)\). Hence, the slope of \(f_t(z)\) is strictly negative in \((-∞, 0)\). Thus, \(σ_t \not\in (-∞,0)\).

15.2 Optimality of Markov policies
For finite horizon models, we have already shown that there is no loss of optimality in restricting attention to deterministic Markov policies. In principle, we can use that result to argue that there is no loss of optimality in restricting attention to Markov deterministic policies for infinite horizon discounted setup as well. However, in this section, we provide an alternative proof, which is useful in its own right.
We start by showing that for any history dependent, possible randomized, policy, there is a randomized Markov policy that has the same performance.

Proposition 15.2 Let \(μ\) be any (possibly randomized) history dependent policy. Then, there exists a time-homogeneous randomized Markov policy \(π\) that has the same performance as \(μ\).







NoteProof





For any policy \(μ\) and initial state \(s^∘\) define the occupation measure \[ ζ(s,a | s^∘; μ)
  \coloneqq \EXP^μ \biggl[ \sum_{t=1}^∞ γ^{t-1} \IND\{S_t = s, A_t = a\}
  \biggm| S_1 = s^∘ \biggr]
\] Then, the performance starting from state \(s^∘\) is given by \[\begin{equation}\label{eq:perf-history}
J(s^∘; μ) = \sum_{s \in \ALPHABET S, a \in \ALPHABET A}
   ζ(s,a | s^∘; μ) c(s,a).
\end{equation}\]
Let \(ζ(s|s^∘;μ) = \sum_{a \in \ALPHABET A} ζ(s,a | s^∘; μ)\) denote the margin of \(ζ\) on \(s\). Note that the performance only depends on states for which \(ζ(s|s ^∘,; μ) &gt; 0\). States where \(ζ(s| s ^∘; μ) = 0\) are never visited under policy \(μ\). Note that \[\begin{align*}
  ζ(s | s^∘; μ)
  &= \EXP^μ \biggl[ \sum_{t=1}^∞ γ^{t-1} \IND\{S_t = s\}
      \biggm| S_1 = s^∘ \biggr]
  \\
  &= \sum_{t=1}^∞ γ^{t-1} \PR^μ(S_t = s \mid S_1 = s ^∘)
  \\
  &= \IND\{s = s ^∘\} + \sum_{t=2}^∞ γ^{t-1} \PR^μ(S_t = s \mid S_1 = s ^∘)
\end{align*}\] Using the law of total probability, we have \[
  \PR^μ(S_t = s \mid S_1 = s ^∘)
  = \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S} \sum_{a_{-} \in \ALPHABET A}
  \PR(s | s_{-}, a_{-}) \PR^μ(S_{t-1} = s_{-}, A_{t-1} = a_{-} \mid S_1 = s ^∘)
\] Therefore, \[\begin{align*}
  \hskip 2em & \hskip -2em
  \sum_{t=2}^∞ γ^{t-1} \PR^μ(S_t = s \mid S_1 = s ^∘)
  \\
  &= \sum_{t=2}^{∞} γ^{t-1}
     \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S} \sum_{a_{-} \in \ALPHABET A}
     \PR(s | s_{-}, a_{-})
     \PR^μ(S_{t-1} = s_{-}, A_{t-1} = a_{-} \mid S_1 = s ^∘)
  \\
  &= γ \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S} \sum_{a_{-} \in \ALPHABET A}
     \PR(s | s_{-}, a_{-})
     \sum_{t=2}^∞ γ^{t-2} \PR^μ(S_{t-1} = s_{-}, A_{t-1} = a_{-} \mid S_1 = s ^∘)
  \\
&= γ \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S} \sum_{a_{-} \in \ALPHABET A}
     \PR(s | s_{-}, a_{-}) ζ(s_{-},a_{-} | s ^∘; μ)
\end{align*}\] Thus, we have \[\begin{equation}\label{eq:flow}
  ζ(s|s ^∘; μ) = \IND\{s = s ^∘\}
  + γ \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S} \sum_{a_{-} \in \ALPHABET A}
     \PR(s | s_{-}, a_{-}) ζ(s_{-},a_{-} | s ^∘; μ)
\end{equation}\] which is the flow equation for occupancy measure.
Now, define a time-homogeneous randomized Markov policy \(π\) as follows: \[π(a | s) = \frac{ ζ(s, a| s^∘; μ) } { ζ(s | s^∘; μ) }\] which implies \[\begin{equation}\label{eq:mu-total}
  ζ(s, a | s ^∘; μ) = ζ(s| s ^∘; μ) π(a | s).
\end{equation}\]
Substituting the above in \(\eqref{eq:flow}\) we get \[\begin{align*}
  ζ(s|s ^∘; μ) &= \IND\{s = s ^∘\}
  + γ \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S} \sum_{a_{-} \in \ALPHABET A}
     \PR(s | s_{-}, a_{-}) π(a_{-}|s_{-}) ζ(s_{-} | s ^∘; μ)
  \\
  &= \IND\{s = s ^∘\}
  + γ \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S}
     \PR_{π}(s | s_{-}) ζ(s_{-} | s ^∘; μ)
\end{align*}\] where \(\PR_{π}(s | s_{-}) = \sum_{a_{-} \in \ALPHABET A} \PR(s|s_{-}) π(a_{-}|s_{-})\). This equation is the same as the policy evaluation equation derived in Proposition 15.1. Therefore, the occupancy measure is given by \[
  ζ^μ = (1 - γ P_{π})^{-1} δ_{s ^∘}
\]
Now, consider the occupancy measure of policy \(π\). Since \(π\) is Markov, we have \[\begin{equation}\label{eq:pi-total}
  ζ(s,a | s ^∘; π) = ζ(s | s ^∘; π) π(a | s)
\end{equation}\] Substituting this in the flow equation \(\eqref{eq:flow}\) for \(π\), we get \[
  ζ(s|s ^∘; π) = \IND\{s = s ^∘\}
  + γ \sum_{s_{-} \in \ALPHABET S}
     \PR_{π}(s | s_{-}) ζ(s_{-} | s ^∘; π).
\] From Proposition 15.1, we have \[
  ζ^π = (1 - γ P_{π})^{-1} δ_{s ^∘}.
\]
Thus, \(π\) and \(μ\) have the same occupancy measure on the state. By \(\eqref{eq:mu-total}\) and \(\eqref{eq:pi-total}\), we get that they have the same occupancy measure on state and action.
Since the performance of a policy depends only on its occupancy measure, \(μ\) and \(π\) have the same performance.

